WEBVTT

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:03.870
JIMMY HERBERT: Once upon a
time, humans wrote software,

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:08.010
humans operated software, and
humans debugged software.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.350
Observability was
built around this.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:14.370
And I'm here to tell you that
our own category is breaking.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:18.000
Logs, traces,
dashboards, alerts,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.420
all of it assumed a human
investigator sitting in front

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.230
of the data trying to
figure out what happened.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.270
But that world is changing.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.330
Now agents are writing
code, operating systems,

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.790
making decisions,
debugging failures,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:35.790
and increasingly interacting
with infrastructure

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:38.280
without a human in the loop.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.710
And, once the consumer
of observability changes

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.570
from humans to agents,

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.560
the assumptions underneath
the category start to fail.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.590
That's the core idea I
want to talk about today.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:54.480
Agentic infrastructure needs
agentic observability.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.500
My name is Jimmy.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.830
I lead the solutions engineering
team here at groundcover.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:00:59.970
And I want to share today

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:03.270
that it's actually not
really a product pitch;

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.730
it's more of a diagnosis.

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:07.890
Something fundamental
stopped working

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:11.180
in observability once
AI systems became part

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.970
of the software
lifecycle itself.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:16.280
And this is not a
feature problem.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.590
It's not a "we need
better dashboards

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:20.930
or more AI features" problem.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.220
This is a systems problem.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.920
The operating assumptions
underneath observability

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.320
are changing.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.480
The first problem is logs.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.000
Logs were built for events.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.090
Agents produce
decision breadcrumbs.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:40.740
Traditional logging assumes
discrete events, stable schemas,

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.020
and human-readable meaning.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.190
request started, request
completed, database failed,

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.610
cache missed, those are events.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.040
But agents don't
behave like that.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.680
An agent retrieves
documents, scores relevance,

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.920
supplies policies,
retries tool calls,

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.610
rewrites outputs,
reevaluates context.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.520
What gets produced is
not an event stream;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.700
it's a reasoning trail.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.490
And that creates a completely
different problem.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:14.610
Now the question isn't
just what happened.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:18.690
The question becomes, why did
the agent make this decision?

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.670
Why did it choose
these documents?

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.860
Why did it block this output?

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:26.370
Why did it retry this
tool five times?

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:29.160
The reasoning happens
across long time horizons

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.500
and wide context windows.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.130
And, honestly, no observability
platform today was really

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.740
designed for this.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.230
Traditional observability
assumed systems were

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.390
mostly deterministic.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.960
You could usually reconstruct
the story afterward.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.160
A request failed.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.200
A dependency timed out.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.990
A deployment introduced latency.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:55.020
Agent systems don't
fail that cleanly.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.860
The same input can produce
different behaviors.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:02.280
The same workflow can
make different decisions.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.360
And, increasingly, understanding
the reasoning path matters more

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.730
than understanding the
infrastructure path.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.340
The second problem is
traces and sampling.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.180
Sampling breaks and
status codes lie.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:23.400
APM was built for a world
that no longer exists.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.370
Let's start with
head-based sampling.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:27.330
A random sample

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:31.530
of a non-deterministic agent
flow tells you almost nothing.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:34.410
The one trace you keep may
completely miss the reasoning

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.020
path that actually mattered.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:40.380
Tail-based sampling isn't
a real answer, either,

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.350
because now you're trying to
hold tens of thousands of spans

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:47.310
in memory waiting to decide
if something failed.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:54.420
At 50,000 spans per session,
that falls apart pretty quickly.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:56.670
And then there's
the bigger issue.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.930
The 200 OK means
absolutely nothing.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:01.890
I'll say it again.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:04.800
200 OK means nothing.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.060
In traditional systems,

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.610
a successful status code
usually meant the system

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:10.440
behaved correctly.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.370
In agent systems, it only
means nothing crashed.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:16.110
The infrastructure succeeded;

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:19.890
the outcome can still
be completely wrong.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:22.920
The agent may have used
the wrong context,

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:26.010
selected the wrong tool,
hallucinated an answer,

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.140
or misunderstood intent.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:32.490
No answer does not
mean correct anymore.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:35.700
These failures are incredibly
difficult to capture

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:39.060
because they're often rare
and nondeterministic.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:42.210
Sampling guarantees you missed
the exact journey you needed

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.400
to debug.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.040
And this changes the
economics completely.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.680
Historically, observability
vendors made money

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:52.770
by charging you to
ingest more telemetry.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:55.020
That worked when telemetry
growth was relatively

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:59.520
predictable, but agent systems
explode telemetry volume.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:02.760
One workflow can generate
thousands of spans,

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.020
hundreds of tool calls,

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.440
and massive amounts
of contextual data.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.980
So now teams are forced
into a bad trade-off:

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:15.270
either keep the data and absorb
the cost, or reduce visibility

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:18.960
and hope the important
signals survive.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.220
Neither option really works.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:31.740
The third problem, sensitivity
and instrumentation.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:36.090
Your telemetry now contains
things you never meant to store:

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:41.250
prompts, customer conversations,
PII, financial information,

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:44.070
internal business logic.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:47.370
Telemetry stopped being
just technical metadata.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:51.540
Now it's potentially the most
sensitive data in the company.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:55.530
That changes the risk
model completely.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:58.680
Shipping all of that to a SaaS
vendor is no longer just a

00:05:58.680 --> 00:05:59.970
cost discussion.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:02.640
It becomes a
liability discussion.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.110
And, at the same time,

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:08.850
manual instrumentation is
collapsing under AI velocity.

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.340
Humans used to maintain
Pipelines manually

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:14.520
because the systems
evolved at human speed.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:17.700
Now AI generates services
and workflows faster

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:22.260
than teams can keep
instrumentation current.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:25.140
So the implications
become pretty obvious.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.990
The observability layer
itself has to learn

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:30.690
to instrument automatically.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:35.580
If you zoom out, all three

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:39.450
of these problems point
to the same thing.

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:42.510
Everything we built in
observability was optimized

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:47.880
for the one thing agents
don't have: human limits.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:51.690
We optimize for less
data, simpler dashboards,

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:55.500
smaller cardinality,
reduced telemetry,

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.230
human readable abstractions.

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:02.370
But agents don't need the same
simplifications humans need.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:06.660
Agents actually improve with
more context, which means a lot

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:11.200
of the old optimizations
become constraints.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.950
This is why I say we
built the whole industry

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.320
for the wrong consumer.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:22.740
For 15 years, observability
tooling was shaped

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:24.960
around human cognition.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.710
Humans can't process
50,000 spans in a session.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.510
Agents can.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:32.580
Humans need summaries.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.100
Agents need full context.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.100
Humans simplify systems
to understand them.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:39.900
Agents often perform better

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:43.710
when they can access the
entire system state.

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:47.130
So right now we're doing
something fundamentally broken.

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:49.710
We're feeding agents a
summary and expecting them

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:52.350
to understand the entire book.

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:56.550
That's not a model problem; it's
a data completeness problem.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.140
And, honestly, I
think this is one

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:01.800
of the biggest misconceptions
in AI infrastructure right now.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.010
A lot of people assume better
models automatically solve

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:08.130
observability, but
smarter models operating

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:12.840
on the incomplete telemetry
still make bad decisions.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:15.330
Context quality
matters just as much

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:19.560
as model quality, possibly more.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:22.050
And this is where I'll make a
slightly controversial point

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:25.140
about MCP style demos.

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:28.080
An external agent
is always a guest.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.120
It doesn't control
the API surface.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:32.040
It doesn't control indexing.

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:34.530
It doesn't control
correlation primitives.

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:36.870
It operates on partial context,

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:39.600
and partial context is
exactly what creates

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:42.010
hallucinated diagnosis.

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:47.250
What agents actually need
is an architecture built

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:50.820
for data abundance, and this
is where groundcover happens

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:53.670
to align very naturally
with this shift.

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:55.560
Agents on groundcover reason

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:59.570
over complete system state,
not a curated slice.

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:01.490
That's an important distinction

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:02.900
because there's a
massive difference

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:05.900
between an agent confidently
hallucinating a fix

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:10.080
and an agent actually
understanding what's wrong.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:13.440
A few architectural bets ended
up mattering a lot here.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:17.760
First, BYOC: Bring
your own cloud.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.250
Everything stays
inside your cloud.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:23.190
That matters for
control, governance,

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:25.080
and sensitive telemetry.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:29.330
Second, zero instrumentation
and zero friction.

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:30.890
You cannot depend on humans

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:36.540
to manually keep instrumentation
current in AI-generated systems.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:38.400
And, third, one platform,

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:41.220
regardless of whether the
code was written by a human

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:46.030
or an agent because,
increasingly, it's both.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:50.930
This leads to a new
model for observability.

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:55.290
Move the LLM up; push
the analysis down.

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:58.050
The model is not the
execution engine.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:00.990
The LLM's job is to
understand intent,

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:03.300
decide investigation strategy,

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:05.580
and generate
deterministic operations

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:08.040
and interpret the results.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:11.610
The back end's job is the
actual analytical work,

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:16.290
close to the data,
accurately, cheaply.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:18.000
That separation matters.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.610
You don't want the model
brute forcing analysis

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.290
over complete context.

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:24.930
You want the model
orchestrating investigation

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:30.660
over a deterministic analytical
system with complete telemetry.

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:32.610
That's a very
different architecture

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:34.980
than what most observability
systems were originally

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:36.730
designed for.

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:41.340
Once you accept that model
investigation changes

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:47.840
completely, investigation starts
from intent, not navigation.

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:49.670
In the old world,
engineers clicked

00:10:49.670 --> 00:10:51.380
through dashboards manually,

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:53.390
trying to piece
together what happened.

00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:56.690
In the new world, you
start with a goal.

00:10:56.690 --> 00:10:58.190
Why did this fail?

00:10:58.190 --> 00:10:59.360
What changed?

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:02.190
Which customers were impacted?

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:04.230
The agent runs the
investigation,

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:07.410
gathers the right signals,
materializes the right outputs,

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:11.670
and returns an answer
plus next actions.

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:14.580
Not a dashboard, an answer.

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:17.250
And this changes
development workflows too.

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:19.770
Right now, teams literally
screenshot dashboards

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:21.900
and paste them into Claude.

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:23.940
That should not exist.

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:28.980
Production telemetry should flow
directly into coding agents

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:32.940
and CI/CD systems as
structured context.

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:35.940
And, eventually, these
systems don't just observe;

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:40.260
they start acting, raising
collection fidelity dynamically,

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:41.910
instrumenting on the fly,

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:44.910
supporting the remediation
during incidents.

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:46.620
And I think this is where
the category starts

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:48.930
to look fundamentally different.

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:53.310
Observability stops being a
passive debugging interface.

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:56.400
It becomes an active
operational system,

00:11:56.400 --> 00:11:58.770
not just showing
humans what happened

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:01.830
but helping systems
understand, respond,

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:04.660
and eventually recover
automatically.

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:09.780
Now, obviously, this
introduces hard questions.

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:12.390
And, honestly, I think the
industry still underestimates

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:16.260
how important these questions
are going to become.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:18.900
How much autonomy should
your observability layer

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:20.760
actually have?

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:24.420
If an agent fixes a production
incident at two in the morning,

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.850
who approved that action?

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:28.530
What guardrails govern systems

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:30.900
that can act directly
in production?

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:33.510
If the agent remediates
incorrectly,

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:35.880
who owns that outcome?

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:39.090
And how do you validate
telemetry normalized correctly

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:42.480
when no human explicitly
defined the schema

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:45.480
because ultimately the
agent's reasoning is only

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:48.010
as good as its data.

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:50.040
I'll say it again.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:53.850
The agent's reasoning is
only as good as its data.

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:55.680
These are not edge cases.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:58.650
These are foundational
operating questions

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:02.020
for the next generation of
infrastructure systems.

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:08.820
My view is that the companies
that get observability right

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:12.510
in the agent era will have
a structural advantage,

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:16.650
not just operationally,
competitively.

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:20.610
AI cannot fix what
it cannot see,

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:24.570
and it cannot build effectively
without full context.

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:27.390
We definitely do not
have every answer yet,

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:29.730
but we know the direction
that this is going.

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:33.510
And we know the questions teams
need to start asking now.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:37.140
Observability is becoming
the operating system

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:40.440
for the agentic SDLC, the
data layer everything else

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:42.600
depends on.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:45.030
This is the shift happening
underneath the industry

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:47.550
right now.

00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:50.910
I'll leave you with the
core idea one more time.

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:54.810
AI can't fix what AI can't see.

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:58.890
The agentic infrastructure
needs agentic observability.

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:00.340
Thank you.
